I've committed the fix for #1214 as 96973df2. The full gate passes: build, test, clippy with -D warnings, and fmt.

What changed:
• `--save-session <path>`: if the path already exists and isn't a saved yoyo session, yoyo now refuses at startup, before any server starts or request is sent. It exits 2, writes nothing, and names the path. "Is a session" uses the same parse that /load and --continue use when loading a session, so I didn't write a second parser. A file that is a session (a re-run or a resume) is replaced as before. The same check runs again just before the write, in case the file appeared during the run.
• `--output/-o`: still replaces an existing file. That's a deliberate choice, because every comparable -o does the same (curl -o, sort -o, gcc -o, shell >). Refusing would break any script that re-runs into the same file. The change is that stderr now says replaced <path> when the file already existed, so the replacement is no longer silent.
• Resume check: no command-line flag loads an arbitrary session file. --continue only reads the auto-save path, and /load only works inside the REPL. So the case that needs "replace a session file" is mainly scripts re-running the same command, and the rule holds for that.

Checks:
• Recall control: I disabled the check in one atomic command (marker counted as 1 before the run). The keep, empty-file and invalid-JSON tests went red by name. After restoring it, the marker count was 0 and the tests were green.
• Built binary, timestamp printed beside the output, aimed at a dead port: the non-session file was refused with exit 2, the file still contained keep, and no request was made. A real session file was replaced. -o over an existing file printed replaced out.txt.
• Test fix along the way: my first version exited the process directly, which tripped the test that bans new direct exits. It now uses the project's dispatch_sub::exit_process helper, and I rebuilt and re-ran the probe after that change.

The before/after table was posted on #1214 before the gate ran. docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md now states both rules and why. --help says nothing about overwriting, so I left it unchanged.

New bug, filed as #1218: if a -p run fails (network, rate limit, bad key), -o answer.md still replaces the file, and leaves it empty. That isn't covered by the -o decision above: a failed run produces no answer, so it shouldn't wipe out the last good one.
